imant Don Carlos (Carlos V) as one of his principal generals during the First Carlist War."}, {"sourceSlug": "miguel-gmez-damas", "sourceName": "Miguel Gómez Damas", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "expedicion-gomez", "targetName": "Expedición Gómez", "context": "Miguel Gómez Damas orchestrated and led the extensive military campaign known as the Expedición Gómez across Spain in 1836."}], "places": [{"name": "Torredonjimeno, Spain", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Córdoba, Spain", "role": "City captured"}, {"name": "Alicante, Spain", "role": "Expedition terminus"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Miguel Gómez Damas", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "miguel-gómez-damas"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q923087", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_G%C3%B3mez_Damas"], "tags": ["Spain", "Military Leaders & Commanders", "Europe", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "empire-and-colonialism"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Miguel Gómez Damas left a notable mark on Early Modern history, documented across 0 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries; Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Miguel Gómez Damas significantly impacted the First Carlist War by prolonging the conflict and forcing major Liberal military responses through his daring trans-peninsular campaigns, notably the Expedición Gómez of 1836.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-24T07:12:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45346013142", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Miguel Gómez Damas was a Spanish Carlist general of the First Carlist War.", "newValue": "Miguel Gómez Damas, born in Torredonjimeno on June 5, 1785, emerged as a pivotal, albeit controversial, Carlist general during Spain's First Carlist War (1833-1840). His military career, marked by audacious maneuvers and strategic daring, made him a formidable opponent to the Liberal forces supporting Queen Isabel II. Operating primarily in southern and central Spain, Gómez Damas embodied the fierce legitimist loyalty to Don Carlos, challenging the established succession and prolonging a brutal dynastic conflict that profoundly shaped 19th-century Spanish politics.

Gómez Damas's most celebrated campaign was the "Expedición Gómez" of 1836, an extraordinary military traverse of the Iberian Peninsula. Leading a force of approximately 5,000 Carlist troops, he embarked from the Basque Country, crossed Castile and Extremadura, and briefly captured cities like Córdoba and Almadén, before reaching the Mediterranean coast at Alicante. Though ultimately failing to ignite a widespread Carlist uprising in Andalusia, this expedition demonstrated a remarkable capacity for sustained offensive action and logistical resilience, forcing significant Liberal troop redeployments and unsettling the Madrid government. His tactics, often characterized by rapid movement and unexpected strikes, kept the Liberal armies in a state of constant pursuit.

Despite the initial successes and the morale boost his expeditions provided to the Carlist cause, Gómez Damas's relationship with the Carlist high command, particularly Don Carlos himself, was often strained. His independent initiatives and occasional insubordination led to his arrest and dismissal in 1837, effectively ending his active military command in the war. Nevertheless, his campaigns had a lasting impact, prolonging the conflict and exposing the vulnerabilities of the Liberal government, contributing to a period of intense political instability and military expenditure that reshaped Spain's internal dynamics for decades to come."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-24T07:12:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45346013142", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1785-06-05, Torredonjimeno"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1785-06-05, Torredonjimeno"}], "newValue": ["The death of King Ferdinand VII in 1833 triggered a dynastic crisis over the Spanish throne, leading to the First Carlist War.", "The promulgation of the Pragmatic Sanction of 1830 allowed female succession, nullifying Don Carlos's claim and fueling legitimist opposition.", "Miguel Gómez Damas's personal loyalty to the absolutist principles represented by Don Carlos drove his military commitment to the Carlist cause."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-24T07:12:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45346013142", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1864-06-11"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1864-06-11"}], "newValue": ["Miguel Gómez Damas's audacious campaigns, particularly the Expedición Gómez, significantly p